Introduction
Many session-filtered Expert Advisors have a hidden vulnerability. It raises no error in the Experts log, and in a backtest it is easy to miss. The EA compiles, deploys, and behaves as expected for months. Then, after a clock change, the session filter that should wait for the London open fires an hour early, or trades that should close before the New York close stay open an extra hour. The cause is rarely the trading logic. It is the relationship between broker server time and real market time, which shifts twice a year with daylight saving time (DST).
Most brokers move their server clock for DST, so the value of TimeCurrent() shifts by one hour. A session hour hardcoded in broker time stays correct only while the market it represents moves its clock together with the broker. The US and the EU do not switch on the same dates. The US moves to DST on the second Sunday of March and back on the first Sunday of November. The EU moves on the last Sunday of March and the last Sunday of October. This creates two mismatch windows: two to three weeks in spring, and exactly one week from the last Sunday of October to the first Sunday of November. In these windows London and New York are 4 hours apart instead of 5, and a filter that is right for most of the year can be one hour off.
This article presents a practical solution from the ground up. It explains why TimeCurrent() alone is not enough for session filtering and walks through the arithmetic of converting broker time to market session time. By the end, you will have a reusable MQL5 include file with the function GetMarketSessionHour(), which computes the session hour at runtime for London, New York, and Tokyo, so you no longer adjust session hours by hand after each clock change. You state the DST schedule and the winter offset of your broker once. The file supports the EU schedule, the US schedule, and a fixed offset.
Section 1: Understanding the Time Problem
What TimeCurrent() Actually Returns
There is a common technical misunderstanding many MQL5 developers have about TimeCurrent(). Many MQL5 developers treat TimeCurrent() as though it represents the actual present time. But in reality, TimeCurrent() denotes the last known server time, which is updated only when a new price tick arrives. TimeCurrent() is broker server time. The value TimeCurrent() returns reflects the timezone the broker's server is configured to operate on.
Relying on the TimeCurrent() function alone to know the real global time (UTC) is not enough. Most brokers manually shift their clock twice a year to stay in line with DST. However, TimeCurrent() is blind to DST changes and will simply return the broker server time when those changes occur. So if a trader hardcodes an EA to trade at 8am UTC without properly accounting for broker time offset, a shift to DST will cause trades to open an hour earlier, causing the trader to miss the intended execution time.
To mitigate this issue, MQL5 provides a TimeGMT() function. TimeGMT() returns the current UTC time. By comparing TimeGMT() (UTC) with TimeCurrent() (server time), the program can determine the broker server's UTC offset.
This solution works well in live trading, but it fails in the Strategy Tester because TimeGMT() and TimeCurrent() both return the same broker server time in the Strategy Tester. If a trader tries to run a backtest using these two functions to calculate the time offset, they will get the wrong answer because both functions are set to the same value; the difference between them is always zero in a backtest.
The DST Calendar Mismatch—Where It Gets Worse
Most traders are aware that clocks change twice a year, but many traders forget that it happens at different times around the world. This gap creates a timing mismatch that affects session filters built around cross-regional pairs.
