When the US and EU Schedules Disagree
Figure 1: DST periods of the US and EU schedules in 2026. The shaded windows, 8 to 29 March and 25 October to 1 November, are the weeks in which the two schedules differ.
The most significant timing risk occurs in March. The US shifts to DST on the second Sunday of March, while the EU shifts to DST on the last Sunday of March, creating a discrepancy that lasts between two and three weeks.
When a trader uses a broker that operates on the US DST schedule, the broker's clock shifts forward by one hour in early March. During this period, a broker on the US schedule jumps from GMT+2 to GMT+3, while the London market remains at 08:00 UTC. Because of this, if a trader has a hardcoded command to trade at '10:00 AM', it executes at 07:00 UTC instead of the correct 08:00 UTC, which is an hour before the London market actually opens.
In late October and early November the mismatch returns. The EU goes back to standard time on the last Sunday of October, while the US stays on DST until the first Sunday of November. This window is exactly one week long. For a broker on the US schedule, the broker is still on DST in that week, but London is already back on standard time, so the London open moves from broker hour 10 to broker hour 11. A hardcoded 10 is then one hour early.
Why the Problem Is Easy to Miss in a Backtest
The historical data of the Strategy Tester carries the time shifts of the broker. A filter that uses a fixed broker hour therefore behaves in the tester as it does in live trading: it trades the same broker hours, and in the weeks of a mismatch it trades a different real session hour. The tester does not hide the shift. It simply does not know which real session you intended.
The effect becomes visible only when you convert the trade times to UTC or to the local time of the market and compare them with the intended session, which most test reports do not do. Without that comparison, the affected weeks look like ordinary variance. News, spreads, rollover, and liquidity can produce the same picture, so check the trade times in UTC before you blame the clock change.
Section 2: Detecting the Broker's DST State at Runtime
The Calculation Strategy
MQL5 has no function that returns the time zone of a broker or its DST state. Two approaches are practical. The first compares TimeCurrent() with TimeGMT(). It shows the current offset in live trading, but it does not work in the Strategy Tester, and it cannot tell the EU schedule from the US schedule for most of the year, because both give the same value. The second calculates the offset from the date, using the calendar rules of a schedule that the trader states. It does not call TimeGMT(), so it works in the same way in live trading and in the Strategy Tester.
This article uses the second approach. The include file does not detect the schedule. It takes two inputs, the DST schedule that the broker follows and the broker's winter offset from GMT, and calculates the offset for any date from them. If either input is wrong, every result is wrong. The script SessionDetect.mq5, described in Section 4 (Determining Your Broker's DST Schedule), estimates the schedule from the history. It also prints the current offset from TimeTradeServer() and TimeGMT() for comparison.
Finding the Nth Sunday of Any Month
The US and EU DST rules are defined in terms of a particular Sunday of a particular month. In the US, the switch happens on the second Sunday of March and the first Sunday of November. In Europe, the switch happens on the last Sunday of March and the last Sunday of October. To calculate these dates, define a function that returns the Nth Sunday for a given month and year.
The logic works as follows: it finds the first day of the target month, computes the day of the week that first day falls on, calculates the gap from the first day to first Sunday, then it adds 7 days increments until it reaches the Nth occurrence. For last Sunday, it finds the first Sunday and keeps adding weeks until the next one would fall outside the month.
