This function is the brain of the entire solution. It takes the time to test, which is the current server time when the argument is omitted, and it reads the DST schedule from the input InpBrokerDST. It does not contain any hardcoded offset values. Every year, it automatically calculates the switch dates using the calendar arithmetic in GetNthSunday(). It works exactly the same in the Strategy Tester and in live trading because it never calls TimeGMT() or TimeDaylightSavings().
A note on names:
In prose, this article uses “DST” for the period in which a schedule has moved the clock forward by one hour, and “winter” for the period outside it. A few identifiers in the code keep the word “summer”: IsBrokerOnSummerTime(), SummerAt(), and the local variables lon_summer and ny_summer. In each case, “summer” means “inside the DST period of the schedule”, not a calendar season. The names are kept as they are because they are short and used throughout the code. IsBrokerOnSummerTime() returns true when a time falls inside the DST period of the selected schedule, and it always returns false for DST_SCHEDULE_NONE.
Section 3: Building the GetMarketSessionHour() Function
The Session Hour Conversion Logic
With the broker DST test working, the next step is converting a broker-time hour into the correct market session hour. The conversion requires two things:  the broker's base UTC offset (e.g., GMT+2 in winter), and the broker's DST status, that is whether the broker is currently on its DST offset or its winter offset.
