On this broker the London open stays at broker hour 10 all year, because London and the broker both follow the EU schedule. The New York open is at 15 for most of the year, but at 14 in the March window and in the last week of October, so a hardcoded 15 is one hour late in those weeks. Tokyo has no DST, so its broker hour changes whenever the broker changes its own clock. A fixed broker hour is therefore safe for one session and wrong for another, which is why the function computes each session on its own.
Session Hours in Broker Time Through the Year
Figure 2: Session hours in broker time for a broker on the EU schedule with a winter offset of 2. The values were calculated with the logic of GetMarketSessionHour() for 15 January, 16 March, and 15 July 2026. The bars run from the session open to the session close. In the March window, the New York session starts and ends one hour earlier.
Since brokers all use different clocks, UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) acts as the common middle ground for the math. To convert a broker's time to real UTC market hours, the code subtracts the broker's offset to find the global UTC time. Once it has the clean UTC time, it simply applies the fixed geographic offset for the specific market you are interested in. New York is always behind UTC, you subtract 5 hours in winter (EST) or 4 hours during DST (EDT), London is usually the same as UTC in winter (+0), but adds an hour (+1) during DST, and Tokyo is consistently 9 hours ahead because it ignores DST entirely.
The terms in this article are used in one fixed way. UTC time is the common reference. Broker time is the server time that TimeCurrent() returns. Market local time is the clock of the city of a session. A session hour always means an hour of broker time, as GetMarketSessionHour() returns it.
The sessions are conventions chosen for this article: London 08:00 to 17:00 London time, New York 08:00 to 17:00 New York time, and Tokyo 09:00 to 18:00 Tokyo time. Other sources define sessions differently, for example by exchange hours or by liquidity windows. To use other hours, change the values in GetMarketSessionHour().
The following code provides an easy-to-use tool that converts global market hours into your broker's local time. By setting the DST schedule and the winter offset of your broker (the winter offset is usually GMT+2) and choosing a market, the function automatically handles the complex DST math and tells you the broker hour in which that session begins.
