Section 4: Using the Include File in a Real EA
Integrating SessionClock.mqh
Integrating the include file into an existing EA involves three steps: first, copy the SessionClock.mqh into the MQL5/Include/SessionClock/ folder, then link it by adding the #include directive at the top of the EA file, and lastly, replace the manual time-check logic in OnTick() with the new function (GetMarketSessionHour() function) call. New inputs for DST and winter offsets now handle time calculations, making the previous GMT offset input obsolete.
//--- At the top of your EA .mq5 file:
#include
<SessionClock/SessionClock.mqh>
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Expert tick function                                             |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
OnTick
()
  {
datetime
now =
TimeCurrent
();
int
lon_open  = GetMarketSessionHour(SESSION_LONDON,
false
,now);
int
ny_close  = GetMarketSessionHour(SESSION_NEWYORK,
true
,now);
MqlDateTime
dt;
TimeToStruct
(now,dt);
int
current_hour = dt.hour;
//--- Allow trading from London open to NY close only
//--- the window can cross midnight in broker time
bool
in_session = IsHourInWindow(current_hour,lon_open,ny_close);
//--- Outside trading window - do nothing
if
(!in_session)
     {
return
;
     }
//--- Your strategy logic continues here...
}
GetMarketSessionHour() returns only a whole hour, so a window whose end is 0 or 1 crosses midnight. For example, with a winter offset of 2 the New York close is hour 0, so the window runs from 10 to 0. IsHourInWindow() handles that case.
The trading strategy does not change. The source of the timing data changes. The trader no longer needs to use fixed, hardcoded numbers that might be wrong when the clocks change, the program now checks for the correct time every single time the market moves (on tick). The EA automatically adjusts for DST so the trader does not have to update their code manually twice a year.  This holds as long as InpBrokerDST and InpBrokerWinter match the broker.
Determining Your Broker's DST Schedule
If you are unsure which schedule your broker follows, the include file cannot tell you, because it only applies the schedule that you state. The script SessionDetect.mq5 estimates it from the history of the server. It assumes that the FX week reopens on Sunday at 17:00 New York time. A server that follows New York shows the first bar of the week at the same server time all year. A server on the EU schedule shows it one hour earlier in the weeks in which the US is on DST and the EU is not. A server with a fixed offset shows it one hour apart between DST and winter.
The script reads H1 bars and takes the first bar after every gap of at least 36 hours, occurring on a Sunday or Monday. It tests 27 candidates: three schedules (none, EU, US) times winter offsets from -3 to 5. For each candidate it converts the weekly open to GMT, then to New York time, and counts the weeks in which the result is 17:00. It reports a result only when the best candidate leads the second one by at least 3 weeks and matches at least 80 percent of the weeks. Use a forex symbol, because CFDs on indexes and commodities do not reopen at that time.
InpDays = 400 covers more than one full year, so that the history holds both periods in which the EU and US schedules differ, which are March and the time from late October to early November, whatever the date of the run. The script stops when InpDays is below 60, and it prints a note when the history holds no week in which the schedules differ. The depth of the history depends on your broker.
