Section 5: Edge Cases and Practical Considerations
The Tokyo Session — No DST, but Still Affected
Japan does not observe DST, which means the Tokyo session always opens at 00:00 UTC and closes at 09:00 UTC. But your broker time offset can affect hardcoded hours targeted at Tokyo sessions. If a broker operates on GMT+2/GMT+3, Tokyo opens at 02:00 broker time in winter and 03:00 broker time during DST. Relying on a function (such as the GetMarketSessionHour() function) that converts UTC to broker time is a reliable way to make sure you trade when the Tokyo banks open.
Session Overlaps and the Two Time Zones Window
Most trading strategies are built around the London-New York Market overlap, which runs from 13:00 to 17:00 UTC (12:00 to 17:00 UTC in the weeks in which only the US is on DST) and represents the highest liquidity period of the trading day. Since both cities have different DST rules, the 'meeting point' of their trading hours can be a moving target. The include file returns the session hours for the date that you pass, so the overlap follows both DST rules, including the weeks in which the US and EU schedules differ. The values are whole hours.
