With the session definitions of this article, New York always opens after London and London always closes before New York, so the overlap runs from the New York open to the London close.
Brokers on Fixed UTC — When DST_SCHEDULE_NONE Is the Right Setting
There are a few brokers that operate on a fixed UTC offset year-round and do not adjust their server time for DST. These brokers explicitly position themselves as 'GMT brokers' or 'UTC brokers.' For these brokers, InpBrokerDST should be set to DST_SCHEDULE_NONE and InpBrokerWinter should be set to the fixed offset of the server, which is 0 for a GMT broker. IsBrokerOnSummerTime() will return false at all times, and GetBrokerOffset() will always return that fixed offset.
Even though the broker's server time does not change, the market-open hours for London and New York markets will appear to move on the broker's chart when the market's country changes its time. By setting the DST schedule to DST_SCHEDULE_NONE, you tell the EA that the broker isn't moving, allowing it to correctly calculate when the US or Europe market opens relative to that frozen GMT clock.
Session-filtering is simpler to manage on these brokers because the broker's clock is fixed year-round. The flip side is that hardcoded session hours in an existing EA that was programmed for a GMT+2/GMT+3 broker will need to be adjusted if the trader migrates to a UTC broker. The include file only needs the two inputs to be changed.
Using the Include File in the Strategy Tester
The entire SessionClock.mqh include file works correctly in the Strategy Tester because it does not call TimeGMT(), which equals the simulated server time in the tester, or TimeDaylightSavings() and TimeGMTOffset(), which depend on the settings of the operating system. All DST calculations are based on TimeCurrent() and calendar arithmetic, both of which are correctly simulated by the tester. The only requirement is that the trader sets InpBrokerDST and InpBrokerWinter to values that reflect the broker's real configuration — the same values they would use in live trading.
When backtesting across a DST switch date, the function switches from the winter offset to the DST offset on the calendar date given by the schedule, with the 02:00 approximation described under Limitations. Table 3 lists the expected values around the 2026 switches for the three supported schedules. Session filters will behave in the backtest as they would in live trading for the same historical period, provided that the two inputs match what the broker used in that period. This is not the case for any approach that relies on TimeGMT() in the tester — those approaches will produce subtly incorrect session timing across every DST boundary in the backtest window.
